body:
Using the Codex agent with the Auto-review permissions preset, the chat transcript fills with one paragraph per auto-approved action. A single turn produced 38 of them, interleaved with the tool calls, each reading like:

> Automatic approval review approved (risk: low, authorization: high): The command only reads repository text to locate the ScriptSure integration, directly supporting the user-requested change, with no mutation, credential access, or data egress.

The actual assistant output is buried between them. On a turn that runs a lot of read-only commands, which is most turns, the transcript becomes almost entirely approval rationale.

## Mechanism

The codex app-server emits a `guardianWarning` notification for each auto-approval decision, including the approvals. `CodexAgent._handleGuardianWarning` forwards the message verbatim as a response part, with no filtering on the decision:

```ts
private _handleGuardianWarning(session: ICodexSession, params: GuardianWarningNotification): ChatAction[] {
	const turnId = session.currentTurnId;
	if (turnId === undefined) {
		this._logService.trace(`[Codex:${session.sessionId}] guardianWarning without active turn; ignoring`);
		return [];
	}
	return [{
		type: ActionType.ChatResponsePart,
		turnId,
		part: {
			kind: ResponsePartKind.SystemNotification,
			content: params.message,
		},
	}];
}
```

`_handleGuardianWarning` is the only producer of `ResponsePartKind.SystemNotification` in `codexAgent.ts`, so every one of these paragraphs comes through this path.

Measured from the AHP log of the affected session (`chat.agentHost` log directory, `ahp/*.jsonl`): 38 `chat/responsePart` actions of kind `systemNotification` whose content begins "Automatic approval review approved", in one session.

## Why this looks unintended rather than by design

The sibling handler for the same feature takes the opposite approach. `_handleGuardianReviewCompleted` returns immediately unless the review was denied:

```ts
if (params.review.status !== 'denied') {
	return;
}
```

Its comment explains that a denial needs a durable Markdown part because the auto-review circuit-breaker can interrupt the turn, leaving the user with no other feedback. So the deliberate position in that handler is that approvals are not worth surfacing and denials are. The `guardianWarning` path never got the same filter, so approvals arrive anyway, through a different door.

## Expected

An auto-approved action should not produce a transcript entry of its own. The approval is already implied by the tool call running, and the rationale, if wanted at all, belongs with the tool call rather than as a sibling paragraph.

Denied reviews should keep their current treatment, which is useful and already handled by `_handleGuardianReviewCompleted`.

## Notes

I have not proposed a patch, because the right filter depends on what `guardianWarning` is meant to carry generally. If it is only ever auto-approval decisions then filtering on the decision in `_handleGuardianWarning` is enough. If it also carries genuine warnings that should reach the user, then the approval notices need to be distinguished from them at the point they are produced, and I cannot tell which from outside.

There is no setting that suppresses these. I checked the `chat.agentHost.codexAgent.*` settings and none gates notification surfacing.

Related: this is one instance of a broader cluster of defects in the agent host chat surface, mapped in #333174.

